/*
  Psychiatric Interviewing Trainer — Study guide, Chapter 8: Nonverbal Behavior
  Written in our own words as a companion to Shea, Psychiatric Interviewing: The Art of Understanding (3rd ed.).
  Clinical exchanges are composed for teaching; they are not transcripts from the book. Book cases are summarized.
  Schema: see the top of guide-ch1.js.
*/
window.TRAINER = window.TRAINER || { parts: {} };
window.TRAINER.guides = window.TRAINER.guides || {};
window.TRAINER.guides.ch8 = {
  chapter: "ch8",
  intro: "Part I closes with the channel that carries most of the meaning. Hall guessed that communication was 10% words and 90% hidden cultural grammar; later research put 60 to 65% of social meaning in nonverbal behavior. Shea's chapter runs in two directions: what you can learn from the patient's body, voice, and use of space, and what your own are doing to the patient without your knowledge. It builds a vocabulary (proxemics, kinesics, paralanguage, immediacy), applies it to spotting hidden psychosis, anxiety, deception, and an unspoken 'no' in the closing, then turns it around to seating, nodding, the guarded patient, note taking, and the patient escalating toward violence. It ends in territory that did not exist for the earlier editions: video, telephone, and text. The single most useful habit it asks of you is to learn your own baseline, on video, with the sound off.",

  highYield: {
    mustKnow: [
      "Two subcategories: nonverbal communications, or [[Emblem|emblems]], carry an agreed meaning within a culture (and a different one in another: the okay sign is vulgar in Brazil, the sole of a shoe an insult to an Arab patient); nonverbal activities (illustrators, regulators, adaptors, affective displays) have no fixed meaning and must be read as hypotheses in context.",
      "Interpret cautiously. A twisted wedding band may mean marital discord, or a patient who usually rolls a pencil. Laughter in a Japanese patient may hide displeasure. The research base is still an adolescent: Western undergraduates, single channels, few replications.",
      "[[Proxemics]] (Hall): intimate 0 to 18 inches, personal to 4 feet, social 4 to 12 feet (most interviews at 4 to 7), public beyond. Felt distance is sensory intensity, not a tape measure: a loud voice or strong odor invades personal space from across the room (the elevator effect). Distances vary by culture, race, and psychological gender.",
      "[[Kinesics]] (Birdwhistell, Scheflen): how a movement is made matters as much as what it is; an abrupt smile from a harried clinician disengages. [[Paralanguage]]: tone, pitch, loudness, rate, fluency; curt 'uh huhs' create a just-the-facts clinician, and silence means privacy, agreement, respect, or 'I'm not done' depending on culture.",
      "[[Immediacy]] is the felt warmth, closeness, and involvement produced by all three fields together, and it has valence. Trusting patients like it; guarded, paranoid, and escalating patients do not.",
      "Context decides meaning: a genuine smile reaches the eyes; the same warm smile reads as hostile to a paranoid patient and as flirtatious to a histrionic one.",
      "Psychosis tends to erode the nonverbal behaviors that maintain social interaction (Sir Denis Hill): long pauses, gaze drifting past you, few regulating gestures, unclear turn-taking, and exaggerated [[Cut-off|cut-offs]] such as hands over the ears. Compare to the patient's baseline.",
      "Anxiety shows in autonomic signs (pallor, sweating, a sudden need for the restroom), in [[Displacement activity|displacement activities]] and auto-contact (each patient has a signature set), in their conspicuous absence (the still-life response), and in fine facial twitches beside the nostril.",
      "Deception clues are mostly clues to the discomfort of lying: a decrease in illustrators, an increase in adaptors, partial or misplaced emblems (a one-shoulder shrug), higher pitch, longer RTL, [[Micro-expression|micro-expressions]]. Below the neck is more reliable than the face. No foolproof method exists; look for patterns that vanish on benign topics and return on sensitive ones. No note taking during suicide inquiry.",
      "[[Incongruent paramessages]] (Grinder and Bandler) reveal ambivalence: 'I hate him' in a resigned voice with palms up. During collaborative planning, closed hands, folded arms, a backward lean, or a hand to cheek or chin mean weighing; 'Are you sure?' only produces a louder yes, so ask about the pros and cons.",
      "[[Kinesic reciprocal|Kinesic reciprocals]] (Scheflen): courting, parenting, dominance. If the patient starts one, the clinician may continue it unawares; refuse the dominance reciprocal with a potentially violent patient.",
      "Engage with your body: sit 4 to 5 feet apart at a slight shared angle, no desk between you (a corner is a dial for immediacy), find each patient's [[Responsive zone|responsive zone]], nod (Shea finds 20% of supervisees underuse it), and match the channel: spoken facilitators for a patient whose head is down. Touch rarely, and only after asking yourself what you intend by it.",
      "Guarded or paranoid: tone every immediacy behavior down. More distance, chairs angled 10 to 45 degrees, more breaks in eye contact, fewer nods and gestures, softer and slower voice, no notes. Sullivan reportedly sat facing the same direction as the patient.",
      "Potential violence: early signs (faster angry speech, sarcasm, pacing, refusing to sit, jabbing finger, staring, rising suspicion) and late signs ([[Intention movements and vacuum gestures|intention movements and vacuum gestures]], threats). Respond with calm voice, no loud or authoritarian tone, fewer displacement activities, less eye contact, hands low and palms up, shoulders stooped and knees bent, more space, approach from the front, chair nearer the door, never turn your back.",
      "Technology: video brings the [[Phantom presence effect]] (no proxemics, weak eye contact, lower alertness; look into the camera every ten seconds, add verbal empathy, trust intuition less); the phone leaves only paralanguage (learn the caller's baseline, increase spoken facilitators, beware humor); text leaves nothing, the [[Naked communication effect]], with new silences and multitasking on both ends."
    ],
    pitfalls: [
      "Nodding at a patient who is looking at the floor, when 'uh huh' and 'go on' were the channel that worked.",
      "Sitting too close by habit, or speaking loudly enough to be inside the patient's personal space from six feet away.",
      "Reading a Latino patient's closer conversational distance as invasive, or a Japanese or Mexican American patient's lowered eyes as poor blending.",
      "Filling a silence that, in the patient's culture, meant 'I'm not finished.'",
      "Taking notes while asking about suicide, incest, or violence, and missing the only sign that something was withheld.",
      "Asking 'Are you sure this is okay?' and believing the more emphatic yes.",
      "Furrowing your brow at a patient's 'tumor' and wondering why he keeps asking whether you understand.",
      "Talking to an elderly, psychotic, or adolescent patient in the slowed, sing-song voice reserved for helpless children.",
      "Trying to calm an escalating patient by moving closer or touching, or hiding your hands behind your back.",
      "Peering at the patient's eyes on the screen, so that from their side you appear to be looking down.",
      "Kidding on the phone with a patient who cannot see the smile that would have carried the joke."
    ],
    phrases: [
      { say: "Uh huh. Go on. Tell me more.", when: "Auditory facilitators for a patient who will not meet your eyes; nods are on the wrong channel." },
      { say: "I'm wondering what is passing through your mind right now.", when: "When the patient shows a visual cut-off (evasive, shifty, stuttering, or stammering eye) or a sudden stillness." },
      { say: "Is there anything more you want to say about how you're feeling? I had the impression you were feeling something more than what you said.", when: "Ekman's questions after a micro-expression or other sign of a withheld emotion." },
      { say: "What are your thoughts on the pros and cons of trying an antidepressant?", when: "A hand to the cheek or a thumb under the chin during the closing: the patient is weighing, so invite the weighing." },
      { say: "I'm going to take a few notes to make sure I remember everything correctly. Is that all right with you?", when: "Beginning rough notes, well into the interview, as a courtesy that also defuses projected paranoia." },
      { say: "Before we start, would you mind if I slide your bed over, so we both have more room to talk?", when: "Setting the space in a crowded hospital room." },
      { say: "It might help you to relax some if you sit over here. Let's sit down and see if we can sort some things out.", when: "An early warning sign: the patient pacing and refusing to sit. If it fails, let them walk, and let someone know you are in there." },
      { say: "You seemed to hesitate a bit before answering that. What was going through your mind?", when: "A silence on the phone, where paralanguage is all you have." }
    ]
  },

  sections: [
    {
      id: "mime",
      title: "The interview as mime",
      lede: "Two interviewers, the same slumped patient, and only one of them on the right channel.",
      blocks: [
        { type: "p", text: "Nonverbal processes touch every way-station on the map: engagement, data gathering, understanding, even diagnosis and the collaborative closing, and they carry cross-cultural cues besides. Shea's opening vignette is an afternoon of supervision in which two young women interviewed the same young man, head pulled to his chest. With the first, blending was weak; an occasional upward nod was all she got. Within five minutes of the second, he sat up, met her eyes, and found some animation in his voice, and by the end a working alliance existed. Both were caring. The difference was channel. The first spoke quietly and nodded often, visual cues wasted on a patient who would not look up. The second used a lively voice punctuated with 'uh huh' and 'go on,' auditory facilitators that reached him, and her nods began to work only as he met her eyes more often." },
        { type: "p", text: "Two lessons follow. Flexibly use different channels depending on what the patient can receive (more vocalizations when the head is down, more nodding with a deaf patient). And, more important, your own nonverbal style has an effect you can learn to alter, which requires first knowing your baseline. The chapter therefore runs in two directions: reading the patient, as Hesse's narrator reads the dark cloud of seriousness crossing a face, and reading yourself." },
        { type: "p", text: "Matsumoto, Frank, and Hwang describe nonverbal behavior as messages sent on many channels at once: face, voice, gesture, posture, distance, touch, gaze. Operationally, it is everything a person displays other than the content of speech. Shea splits it in two. Nonverbal communications, Ekman and Friesen's [[Emblem|emblems]], carry an agreed meaning: a raised finger, a referee's signal, a raised hand in class. They are easy to read with one caveat that bites in the first interview: cultures assign different meanings to the same emblem. The American okay sign is vulgar in Brazil, zero in France, a rude sexual gesture in Arab countries; crossing an ankle over a knee shows the sole of the foot, a deep insult to an Arab patient even if he knows what Americans mean by it. Clinicians new to a region or an immigrant community should ask experienced colleagues about the local emblems at once." },
        { type: "list", items: [
          "[[Illustrators and regulators|Illustrators]]: hand gestures that complement speech, pointing (deictic) or outlining size and shape (iconic). Sign language partly grew from them.",
          "[[Illustrators and regulators|Regulators]]: the eye contact, nods, and hand positions that control the flow of conversation, Bente's dialog functions: when to speak, when to stop, whether one is understood.",
          "[[Adaptor|Adaptors]]: mostly unconscious comfort behaviors, from stroking the face and rolling a pen to shifting in the chair. Multiply determined: a tired body, social discomfort, anxiety, or deceit.",
          "[[Affective display|Affective displays]]: spontaneous facial movements that accompany emotion; the prominent ones are nearly universal, and reading the subtle ones is a learnable skill (Ekman's Emotions Revealed)."
        ] },
        { type: "pearl", title: "A cautionary note", text: "Nonverbal activities are multiply determined. The woman twisting her wedding band may be in marital discord, or intimidated by the interviewer and missing her usual pencil. Laughter in a Japanese patient may cover displeasure, embarrassment, or shock, so a clinician who reads it as blending may never see the patient again. 'Body language' promises more codification than exists. The research, vast and promising, is still young: Western undergraduate samples, variables studied in isolation when they act jointly, and few replications. Treat this chapter as the considered opinion of a craft, which Shea and his trainees have nevertheless found invaluable." }
      ],
      practice: [{ kind: "quiz", id: "ch8", label: "Quiz: Chapter 8" }, { kind: "practice", id: "c8p1", label: "Practice: matching the channel" }]
    },

    {
      id: "fields",
      title: "Three fields: proxemics, kinesics, paralanguage",
      lede: "Space, movement, and voice, each with its own founder and its own cultural traps.",
      blocks: [
        { type: "p", text: "Edward T. Hall coined [[Proxemics]] in The Hidden Dimension: the study of how people are affected by the distances between themselves and others. Humans protect territories, and each distance evokes different feelings, expected behaviors, and sensory channels. At the intimate distance (0 to 18 inches) touch, smell, and warmth dominate and vision blurs; at the personal distance (to 4 feet) sight gains importance; the social distance (4 to 12 feet) is the playground of voice and eyes where most interviews happen, at 4 to 7 feet; at public distance (12 feet and beyond) people lose individuality. Strangers who encroach on intimate or personal space provoke awkwardness and resentment, so begin about 4 to 6 feet away, and extroverted clinicians should watch their habit of sitting close." },
        { type: "p", text: "Patients do not measure with yardsticks. Sensory intensity creates felt distance: a loud clinician invades personal space from six feet, and a patient who seldom bathes, or who wears too much perfume, invades everyone's intimate zone from across the room, which is why such patients draw resentment. A clinician who intrudes sets off the elevator effect: eye contact drops, movement stops, conversation thins, as if the interview were happening in a crowded lift. Culture shifts the distances. Arab students speak louder, stand closer, touch and gaze more; Latinos, Africans, and Indonesians converse closer than Anglos, so a White clinician who backs away may read as cold, and a patient at the culturally correct distance may be misread as invasive. Black Americans may prefer more distance than White Americans; male-female pairs sit closest, male-male farthest; psychological gender predicts comfort better than biological sex." },
        { type: "p", text: "[[Kinesics]] is the study of the body in motion: gestures, limbs, face, eyes (blink, gaze, pupils), posture, and why. Birdwhistell founded it in 1952, pioneered video microanalysis, and coined the kine; his student Scheflen showed that movement often regulates others' behavior, as with the regulators that decide who speaks. The newer emphasis is on implicit qualities, how a movement is made: speed, acceleration, complexity, symmetry. An abrupt smile from a rushed clinician in the waiting room can be more disengaging than engaging. Culture again: Kuhnke's Maria asked her Japanese colleague whether he was pleased with their work and was told yes; days later she learned he was not. 'But I told you with sad eyes, Maria.' Freud: if his lips are silent, he chatters with his fingertips." },
        { type: "p", text: "[[Paralanguage]] (also vocalics) is how words are delivered: tone, loudness, pitch, rhythm, fluency. 'That was a real nice job in there' is a compliment or a sneer depending entirely on delivery. Speech disturbances (stutters, slips, repetitions, omissions, 'ah' and 'um') occur about once per sixteen words and rise sharply under stress, a running anxiety gauge. Facilitators like 'uh huh' depend on how they are said: a warm, upbeat clinician whose patients kept shutting down turned out on video to fire quick, slightly sharp 'uh huhs,' 'yeps,' and 'yeahs' that, with heavy note taking, created a just-the-facts persona. Silence is the cultural minefield: Americans find it negative; the English and Arabs use it for privacy; Russians, French, and Spanish read it as agreement; in Asian cultures it signals respect, and for many Chinese and Japanese it means 'I have more to say,' so a counselor who fills it cuts the patient off and, worse, imputes false motives." }
      ],
      practice: [{ kind: "sim", id: "sim-pendant", label: "Interview: The pendant (reading the room)" }]
    },

    {
      id: "immediacy",
      title: "Immediacy and context",
      lede: "The sensation of being in another person's presence, and the constellation that decides what any one cue means.",
      blocks: [
        { type: "p", text: "[[Immediacy]] is the sensation of being in the immediate presence of another person and the warmth, closeness, involvement, and acceptance (or their opposites) created and reflected by nonverbal behavior. It is the nonverbal core of what Chapter 1 called blending, and Peter Andersen argues it is the central function of nonverbal behavior. All three fields feed it: how close we sit and how far we lean (proxemics), emblems, illustrators, regulators, adaptors, and affective displays (kinesics), and tone, loudness, and pace (paralanguage). Eye contact contributes, and even pupil size: in Hess and Goodwin's study, retouching a mother's pupils larger made viewers judge that she loved her baby more, a trick advertisers still use. Smiles, nods, gestures, synchrony, and above all touch convey immediacy, which is why touch demands caution." },
        { type: "pearl", title: "Immediacy has valence", text: "Exactly as with empathic statements, immediacy behaviors range from low to high valence, and each has its place. High-valence immediacy engages a trusting patient and unsettles a paranoid or escalating one. This single principle underlies the two hardest applications later in the chapter." },
        { type: "p", text: "Context decides meaning. A friend's raised finger after a playful insult is a joke because of the smile, the twinkle, and the tone around it. Ekman's elements of context are the nature of the conversation, the history of the relationship, whether the behavior occurs while speaking or listening, and its congruence with simultaneous cues. Congruence may be the most important: a genuine smile moves the muscles around the eyes, narrows the lids, and often comes with a gentle nod, while the smiles of anxiety, discomfort, contempt, or deceit stop at the mouth. Psychopathology is context too: the clinician's genuine warm smile is hostile to a patient with paranoid process and flirtatious to one with histrionic structure." }
      ]
    },

    {
      id: "hidden-pathology",
      title: "Assessment: nonverbal hints of hidden psychopathology",
      lede: "In psychosis, the nonverbal behaviors that maintain social interaction tend to be lost.",
      blocks: [
        { type: "p", text: "Sir Denis Hill, in the 1972 Maudsley Lecture, recalled psychiatrists who could predict a patient's mental state from the first minutes before a word was exchanged, and feared the skill was dying. His key observation: unlike neurotic states, psychotic states erode the nonverbal behaviors that maintain social interaction. Knowing this lets a clinician probe for psychosis that is otherwise subtle." },
        { type: "case", title: "Mary", text: "Summarized from the chapter. A high-functioning secretary in her mid-twenties, urged to assessment by her sister and a friend while her mother is hospitalized for depression. After fifty minutes a talented trainee recognized a mood disorder but missed its severity and was about to arrange outpatient follow-up. Her body had been saying otherwise. Shea's immediate second interview uncovered a weekend of psychotic terror: she believed her dead father had returned to murder her, told her small siblings, and fled the house, and even in the room could not quite say it was impossible. The cues: pauses of four to eight seconds before answers, slowed and flattened speech, a distracted gaze that drifted past the interviewer whether talking or listening (the normal eye duet of glancing away to begin and back to finish was missing), hands locked and still so that no one could tell when she would start or stop speaking, and, as questions led her back toward her fears, a slow lean forward until her head was cupped in her hands with her palms over her ears, all eye contact gone, for a full five minutes while she answered slowly and cooperatively." },
        { type: "p", text: "That last posture is a [[Cut-off]], an adaptor that dampens environmental stress. Exaggerated to social inappropriateness, cut-offs can mark psychotic process; catatonic withdrawal is a prolonged, drastic one. Measured against Mary's baseline social competence, hers was profoundly deviant, and a friend later reported her staring at her office phone for long stretches. Mild cut-offs are everyday and still informative. Morris names four visual ones: the Evasive Eye (studying an invisible object on the floor, typical of a disengaged adolescent), the Shifty Eye (glancing away and back), the Stuttering Eye (lids fluttering as if swatting the gaze away), and the Stammering Eye (an exaggerated blink of several seconds). They may mean the patient no longer wants to communicate, often because a topic has touched a nerve, and a gentle 'I'm wondering what is passing through your mind right now' can open it; they are also objective signs of a shut-down forming, and exaggerated versions appear in histrionic presentations." },
        { type: "p", text: "Research on diagnosis-specific nonverbals is tentative and mostly confirms common sense. Schizophrenia: gaze aversion, flattened affect with diminished eyebrow movement (which may also be medication), slumped posture, a lean away. Agitated depression: puzzled expression, grimacing, gaze aversion, crouched posture leaning toward the interviewer; withdrawn depression: more gaze, slowed movement, self-touching, blank expression, a lean back. The useful corollary is for follow-up: the return of ordinary hand gesturing, spontaneous expression, and smiling may herald remission before the patient admits to feeling better." },
        { type: "list", items: [
          "The adolescent with curly reddish hair who plunked down beside the resident on the couch, arm briefly along the back of it behind his shoulder, knee inches from his body, bright and snappy: an incursion into near-intimate space within seconds. He leaned away and crossed his legs. Not proof of anything, but a reason to explore histrionic and borderline traits.",
          "The late-middle-aged woman kept waiting too long, who met the apology with cool eyes and no smile, then slowly stretched her legs out until her feet bumped his, and did not withdraw. He did. Passive-aggressive traits were worth exploring, and the history later supported the hunch.",
          "The tall woman in black who pulled up a second chair as a footstool, stretched out, cut the female interviewer off with the Evasive Eye, mumbled through a dour mask, and kept her coat on her lap all hour. Antisocial, borderline, narcissistic, or passive-aggressive leanings all deserved a look. None of this is diagnosis; it tells you where to spend the limited time of the body. And never mistake a cultural difference for a hint of pathology."
        ] }
      ]
    },

    {
      id: "anxiety-deception",
      title: "Assessment: anxiety, deception, and ambivalence",
      lede: "The autonomic system does not lie. Everything else is a hypothesis.",
      blocks: [
        { type: "p", text: "Sympathetic activation prepares fight or flight: faster heart, blood shunted from skin and gut to muscle (the pallor of someone who has seen a ghost), dry mouth, faster breathing, sweat. Shea's own example is a medical student sweating over a bratty nine-year-old until a drop fell on the boy's chest ('You're sweatin' all over me!'); the student blamed a thyroid condition, and the student was Shea. If the sympathetic surge finds no outlet, a parasympathetic rebound follows, which is why a patient who opens by asking for the restroom may be more anxious than they admit. Anxiety that reflects poor blending can be addressed; anxiety around a topic can be probed." },
        { type: "p", text: "Morris's [[Displacement activity|displacement activities]] release tension: the businessman unpacking and repacking his briefcase, smoking, hair twirling, nail biting, ring play, foot twitching, earlobe tugging, grooming, tearing cups, biting pens (and cocktail drinks, which give hands something to do). Each patient has a signature set; once decoded, a sudden increase is a more reliable sign than face or words that an interpretation landed or a topic hurts. Auto-contact behaviors, Morris's self-intimacies, are unconsciously mimed acts of being touched: jaw support, chin support, hair clasp, cheek support, mouth touch, temple support (hair touching three to one women, temple two to one men), hugging one's own knees, in regressed patients a fetal curl. One patient pulled her hair across her cheek and sniffed it. Anxiety can also show as the conspicuous absence of adaptors, the still-life response of someone trying to make no mistakes; it afflicts interviewers on video more than patients, which supervisors should remember. And watch for fine muscle twitches beside the nostrils and on the cheeks: a bright businesswoman who recounted her life only from age sixteen showed them by her left nostril when asked about her father, and burst into tears when Shea named the feeling." },
        { type: "p", text: "Deception cues are mostly cues to the anxiety, embarrassment, or fear of lying. In Ekman and Friesen's foundational study, nursing students described gory films as pleasant; deceivers attended to their faces, so the body below the neck leaked more. Ekman's later work on [[Micro-expression|micro-expressions]], facial movements of 1/15 to 1/25 of a second betraying concealed anger, sadness, or disgust, holds that observers can be trained (his METT tool); they reveal that something is withheld, consciously or by repression, not why, which matters most when suicidal intent, incest, or partner violence is being hidden. His two follow-up questions: 'Is there anything more you want to say about how you are feeling?' and 'I had the impression you were just feeling something more than what you said?'" },
        { type: "list", items: [
          "Partial or misplaced emblems: a one-shoulder shrug, a single palm turning up, an angry fist appearing quietly at the side.",
          "A drop in illustrators and regulators, especially for an unrehearsed lie; a sudden stillness of the hands during the drug history in a normally animated patient.",
          "A rise in adaptors, the best-supported correlate across reviews: fidgeting, mouth covering, nose touching (the popular 'Pinocchio response' lacks research support, though President Clinton touched his nose 26 times during sensitive grand-jury questions and not during mundane ones).",
          "Paralanguage: higher pitch (also fear), longer RTL, longer answers. But a pause followed by self-promotion reads as deceit and a pause followed by self-deprecation as candor.",
          "Strategy: note the patient's behavior after the suspect topic passes, return to it later, and watch for the pattern to vanish on benign ground and reappear on sensitive ground, repeatedly."
        ] },
        { type: "pearl", title: "Andersen's three points, and Shea's two", text: "No foolproof nonverbal lie detector exists or will; some commonalities nonetheless help; and a mix of unconscious and strategic processes produces subtle changes during deception. Practically: learn each patient's baseline early, and during sensitive inquiry such as suicide assessment put the pen and keyboard down, because note taking blinds you to the only warnings there may be and robs you of your own eye contact." },
        { type: "p", text: "Mixed messages are not always deceit; they are often ambivalence. Grinder and Bandler called the simultaneous messages on each channel paramessages, congruent when they agree and incongruent when they conflict. People who habitually communicate incongruently make others uneasy, and incongruence can point to mixed feelings the patient does not know they have. Ms. Davis, in her early thirties, bereaved of the mother she had served as caretaker and verbal whipping post, said of the boyfriend who enjoyed her sexually but would not marry her: 'I hate him, I'll never go back to him.' But the voice was pained resignation, a child's pout rather than indignation, the breathing never quickened, and the hands lay low with palms up, supplication rather than accusation. All the paramessages were true: anger, and a near-masochistic need for his acceptance, the first clue to a hostile dependence that proved central in therapy and identical to her bond with her mother." }
      ],
      practice: [{ kind: "practice", id: "c8p5", label: "Practice: note taking during sensitive topics" }]
    },

    {
      id: "planning-scripts",
      title: "Assessment: the unspoken 'no' and kinesic reciprocals",
      lede: "'Are you sure this is okay?' produces a louder yes. The hands already told you.",
      blocks: [
        { type: "p", text: "In the closing, agreement is easy to read when it is real: natural eye contact, excitement in the voice, a forward lean, nods. The problem is the verbal yes with unspoken misgivings, understandable in a patient in pain, with no time to reflect and too much information arriving in a strange setting. 'How do you feel about these ideas?' is good collaborative practice but sometimes fails, because a hesitant patient who likes the clinician feels social pressure to seem cooperative. 'Are you sure this is okay?' fails worse: Shea has watched ambivalent patients answer it with 'Oh yeah, this sounds like a good plan,' because the question raised the fear of being seen as difficult." },
        { type: "p", text: "Kuhnke's discordant signals help. Relaxed, on-board patients gesture often with open palms; hesitant ones close their fingers over their palms wherever their hands rest, fold their hands to hold in tension, fold their arms or cross their ankles while listening intently, and lean back. These signs beside a verbal yes mean real doubts, worth uncovering because the patient may have good reasons the clinician missed, and because a plan the patient does not like will not work. Three adaptors signal active weighing: hand to cheek, chin resting on a hand, and a thumb under the chin with the index finger up the side of the face. That is the moment to ask directly about the weighing: 'What are your thoughts on the pros and cons of trying an antidepressant?' It opens communication to a surprising degree." },
        { type: "p", text: "Scheflen observed that humans, like other animals, run shared behavior patterns that escalate toward an act (fighting, mating, parenting) and that mutually recognized displays often make the act unnecessary. He called the patterns [[Kinesic reciprocal|kinesic reciprocals]], and if a patient begins one, the clinician may continue it unawares. The courting reciprocal: a videotape of a young woman leaning pertly forward with a cigarette and her therapist lounging rakishly back with his, voices spritely and coy, looked like the opening of a romantic comedy; an erotic transference and a false impression of histrionics are the likely products. A talented trainee, puzzled that male patients asked her out after evaluations, saw mild flirtatiousness on her own tape and removed it. Scheflen's catalogue (men straightening to full height, jutting the jaw, pulling in the belly, standing close; women tilting the head, glancing from the corner of the eye, lifting the upper body, crossing legs to show the calf, presenting palms up while pushing back hair) is dated in tone but the dance persists, including tentative incursions into intimate space and the first touch." },
        { type: "case", title: "The microphone", text: "Summarized from the chapter. A patient just turned twenty, eyes often downcast, hands the young interviewer a folded note to read first. Her voice steps meekly from her lips. She fumbles with the lapel microphone, correctly around her neck but unattached; asked if she needs help, she keeps fumbling, then, head cocked down, glances up with the helpless eyes of a little girl and nods without a word. The clinician leans over and fixes it. The parenting reciprocal, enacted as naturally as between mother and child in the first minutes of the scouting period, pointed to character pathology; further interviewing found a mixed personality disorder with histrionic, passive-aggressive, and dependent features, and a perfected art of eliciting parental responses." }
      ],
      practice: [{ kind: "practice", id: "c8p4", label: "Practice: reading a weighing gesture" }]
    },

    {
      id: "engaging",
      title: "Engaging with your body: seating, the responsive zone, touch, and nodding",
      lede: "Ninety percent of trainees, left to themselves, sit the same way. The desk belongs at a corner, not in between.",
      blocks: [
        { type: "p", text: "In Shea's class, two trainees on rolling chairs are told to arrange themselves for comfortable conversation. About 90% end up 4 to 5 feet apart, turned toward each other but 5 to 10 degrees off the direct line, both in the same direction (Figure 8.1A). Turned to face head-on, they report feeling too close; eye contact is forced and cannot be broken without a large head movement, and the position feels confrontational. The angled position allows good eye contact and easy release, and it does something more: directly facing, the fields of vision barely overlap ('you are there, I am here'); angled, they overlap ('we are here together, and the world is out there'), a phenomenological boost to blending (Figure 8.1B). Some experts prefer the oppositional arrangement; Shea does not. Each clinician and each dyad will find their own best position." },
        { type: "p", text: "A desk between clinician and patient is a chief executive's arrangement, not a therapist's. Placed with only a corner protruding (Figure 8.1C), and with a wheeled chair, the desk becomes a dial: roll back and more desk intervenes for a paranoid patient who needs a protective barrier; roll forward until none does for a well-engaged one; and a clipboard or laptop moves from lap to desk unobtrusively. The office is an extension of the clinician; even a trainee's room can be rescued from prison-cell austerity by a few posters and plants. In a crowded hospital room, ask to slide the bed over." },
        { type: "p", text: "Within the office lies the [[Responsive zone]]: the distance at which the patient feels their personal space respected while the clinician's movements still register. Closer frightens; farther, and a gentle forward lean at a sensitive moment has no effect. The zone is larger for a paranoid patient (start farther away, or with a desk corner between, and close the distance as blending grows) and smaller for a withdrawn, depressed elderly patient during the cognitive examination, where sitting closer, speaking louder, and asking the patient to watch you say the digits may prevent an artificially low score and a false dementia." },
        { type: "pearl", title: "Two questions before touching", text: "Touch is the most powerful immediacy behavior, instantly entering the intimate zone, part of the courting reciprocal, easily misread by psychotic or histrionic patients, and harshly disengaging to the paranoid or angry. Before touching, ask: what do I want to accomplish, and is this intentional interviewing or habit or my own need? And does touch fit this patient's immediacy needs and this moment's context? Shea rarely touches in a first interview beyond a handshake, but a hand on the arm can bring a withdrawn patient's eyes up for the cognitive exam ('I know it's hard to concentrate right now, but it really is important'), and when a kind mill worker, brain-damaged by an industrial poison, wept and begged not to be thought stupid, grasping his arm was the natural thing. A clinician who routinely needs to touch should ask why; the wish to be a comforting angel, flirtatiousness, or histrionic traits can open the door to misconduct." },
        { type: "p", text: "The spontaneous appearance of immediacy behaviors in the patient monitors blending as the DOU does: better eye contact, more talking with the hands (a hint, in a shut-down interview, to pursue the current topic), turning toward you, asymmetric relaxed posture rather than tense symmetry. Keep culture in view: African American listeners may show attention by presence rather than gaze; direct eye contact may be disrespectful for Mexican American and Japanese patients, so poor eye contact there is not rudeness, boredom, or weak blending. [[Postural echoing]], well-blended people adopting matching postures and shifting and relaxing together, is both a sign that engagement is on track and a technique: cross your leg ankle-over-knee with the steelworker who sits that way, and you metacommunicate 'we are not as different as one might suppose.'" },
        { type: "p", text: "Persuasive counselors keep eye contact, smile, gesture, and lean about twenty degrees forward. The head nod means yes in every culture studied, including among the deaf, the blind, and people incapable of speech, and comes in flavors: acknowledgment, encouragement, understanding, agreement, fact. Learn your own frequency; skilled engagers nod many times in any few minutes, and about 20% of Shea's supervisees underuse it, a few barely at all. When Shea experimentally stopped nodding with a long-term patient, the man broke off within two minutes: 'What's wrong? Somehow I feel you don't like what I'm saying.'" }
      ],
      practice: [{ kind: "practice", id: "c8p1", label: "Practice: matching the channel" }]
    },

    {
      id: "guarded",
      title: "The guarded or paranoid patient, and knowing your own voice and face",
      lede: "Paranoid patients don't want immediacy. Giving it anyway builds the paranoid spiral.",
      blocks: [
        { type: "p", text: "Chapter 1 showed that guarded and paranoid patients do better with low-valence empathic statements, or none, since high intuition suggests the clinician is inside their head. Shea has come to believe nonverbal behavior matters even more. The golden principle is simple to state and odd to practice: tone down immediacy. Proxemically, set the chairs farther apart, and when paranoia shows, wheel back so more desk corner intervenes. Break eye contact more often; the attentive gaze of a good listener becomes the stare of a persecutor, so give visual space and angle the chairs 10 to 45 degrees off the direct line; Sullivan, by lore, sometimes sat facing the same way as the patient. Nod and gesture less; the intoxicated man in the emergency room who mocked Shea's nods with a jutting jaw and grunted 'uh huhs' later attacked a guard, and a patient who answers your engaging behaviors negatively may be guarded, hostile, or dangerous. Speak more softly and slowly. And take no notes." },
        { type: "p", text: "All of which requires knowing your own paralanguage. Shea's is upbeat, slightly pressured, slightly loud; he begins every interview at a calmer middle ground and adjusts from there, and on hectic days catches the speed-up before entering the waiting room. One trap deserves its own warning: the slowed, sing-song voice clinicians unconsciously adopt with the elderly, as if addressing a helpless child, and sometimes with psychotic patients and adolescents. It reads as condescension." },
        { type: "case", title: "The furrowed brow", text: "Summarized from the chapter. A boy at the start of adolescence, worried as a sixty-year-old in agitated depression, self-referred and unwilling to have his mother contacted, fixated since a cancer documentary on a small bump on his ear that he was sure was a tumor. He grew more uneasy as the interview went on and kept asking, 'You don't understand, do you?' The clinician's reassurances did not help, because each time the tumor came up his brow furrowed into two vertical lines, read by the boy as disbelief or condemnation. The clinician had no idea; the habit cropped up in all his interviews. He was unconvinced by supervision until, weeks later, a patient stopped mid-interview to ask why he was frowning." },
        { type: "p", text: "Two exercises build self-awareness: picture a mirror descending between you and the patient during the interview, a rude reminder that every move is on display; and watch yourself on video, which is the only reliable way to catch habits like the furrowed brow. Your own displacement activities (Shea twists his hair behind his ears) are a monitor of your anxiety, which may come from matters at home, from countertransference, or from intuitively registering hostility or hidden psychosis; and a few are worth eliminating because they distract or misread (hair twisting may look effeminate to some patients). Escalating and paranoid patients, hyperattentive to the environment, are unsettled by a nervous clinician; one paranoid man asked Shea why he had just scratched his head and was not reassured to hear it itched. Smoking, including the Freudian pipe, is a distraction or irritant that patients may not feel free to object to. And then there is the displacement activity one trainee named at once: 'I'm constantly scribbling notes.'" }
      ],
      practice: [{ kind: "practice", id: "c8p2", label: "Practice: lowering immediacy" }, { kind: "sim", id: "sim-watching", label: "Interview: Watching (a paranoid opening)" }]
    },

    {
      id: "notes",
      title: "A few notes on note taking",
      lede: "Rough notes for recall, as few as you need. The record is written after the patient leaves.",
      blocks: [
        { type: "p", text: "Much note taking in initial interviews is a displacement activity that distracts both parties, and a clinician looking at a keypad cannot attend to the nuances this chapter describes. Shea separates rough notes (fragments typed or jotted to jog memory) from the finalized electronic record, written, typed, or dictated after the patient leaves. The record is the main way of passing information, some of it life-saving, to future clinicians, and it will be read line by line by opposing counsel in any suit; it needs nearly full attention. So does the interview: engaging, uncovering valid data, reading nonverbals, monitoring one's own dynamics, formulating, planning. Neither can be done while composing polished paragraphs, and every second spent transcribing is stolen from the patient's hour. The interview is a human event; the record is a mechanical one; each gets its own time." },
        { type: "list", items: [
          "Minimize rough notes too: dates, doses, treatment histories, family trees.",
          "Little or none in the scouting phase beyond demographics; patients respond to clinicians interested in them rather than in a clipboard.",
          "None at all while raising or exploring suicide, incest, or domestic violence. Put the clipboard or laptop on the floor.",
          "Begin late, and ask: 'I'm going to take a few notes to make sure I'm remembering everything correctly. Is that all right with you?' It is courteous and deflates the paranoia patients project onto note taking. No notes with actively paranoid patients.",
          "Take as many as you need to write an accurate record later, which depends on your memory and on whether you can write it immediately or at day's end.",
          "The fields of the EHR must never dictate the order of your questions; Chapters 3 and 4 own the structure."
        ] },
        { type: "p", text: "A resident taught Shea the best laptop technique: feet on the floor, laptop open on her lap, typing only rough notes, perhaps 10 to 15% of the time, and whenever she stopped she partly closed the lid so the patient could see she could not reach the keyboard and leaned slightly forward over it, metacommunicating 'I'm interested in listening to you'; for longer stretches she set it aside. Many fast typists prefer a clipboard for the freedom to sit and lean, then type the record from it afterward, as Shea does. Either works; find yours." }
      ],
      practice: [{ kind: "practice", id: "c8p5", label: "Practice: note taking during sensitive topics" }]
    },

    {
      id: "violence",
      title: "Calming a potentially violent patient",
      lede: "Violence is a dyadic process. The anthropologist survived the baboons by refusing the dominance reciprocal.",
      blocks: [
        { type: "p", text: "Tardiff reports that about 17% of psychiatric patients in emergency departments are potentially violent and that roughly 40% of psychiatrists have been assaulted at least once. Violence is a two-person system, and clinicians can escalate an agitated patient with their own nonverbals, a violence reciprocal in Scheflen's terms, which can usually be broken. Shea's anthropology professor told of a field researcher who startled a mother baboon, drew the guard males, and saved himself by reproducing the troop's submissive gestures, lowering himself and moving his jaw. Humans too have displays of intent to attack and intent to submit, and reading the first while using the second can defuse many encounters, though not all; the aim is to lower the odds." },
        { type: "p", text: "Anticipate. Most psychotic patients are not violent, but simmering paranoid delusions, mania, organic states (frontal syndromes, delirium, dementia), and intoxication with alcohol, speed, bath salts, hallucinogens, or PCP raise the odds; alcohol is the great instigator and is easy to underestimate because we drink socially. A history of assault calls for safety officers unobtrusively nearby. Involuntary commitment is always a dangerous situation: a calm, seated woman, family beside her, officers present, seized a clinician by the throat the moment she turned her back to walk to the staff room. Never turn your back on a patient in an emergency department. And separate feuding family members quickly, before it is too late, with different staff calming each." },
        { type: "compare",
          a: { title: "Early warning signs", items: ["Faster speech with an angry edge; sarcasm and challenges ('You think you're a big shot')", "Pacing and refusing to sit", "Rapid, jerky gestures; a finger jabbed at you to make a point", "Intense staring", "Rising suspiciousness or disorganization", "Easy to ignore when you are rushed, which is exactly when ignoring them escalates the patient"] },
          b: { title: "Late warning signs", items: ["[[Intention movements and vacuum gestures|Intention movements]]: clenched fists, white knuckles on an object, lips pulled back from the teeth, a closed fist raised overhead (the instinctive overhand blow of brawls and children)", "[[Intention movements and vacuum gestures|Vacuum gestures]]: shaking a fist, a boxing stance, strangling motions, a fist pounded into a palm", "Verbal threats or statements of intent to strike"] } },
        { type: "p", text: "At the early signs, crystallize what the patient needs and move with it. Ask the pacing patient to sit, gently then firmly ('It might help you to relax some if you sit over here'; 'I'd like you to sit over here so we can talk'; 'It's hard to keep staring up'), and if that fails let them walk, recognizing impaired impulse control. Make an excuse to leave the room and tell someone you are in there; it is easy now and may not be in ten minutes. Know where the safety button is." },
        { type: "steps", items: [
          "Appear calm; a normal, unhurried voice, never loud or authoritarian. Fear makes clinicians raise their voices, speed up, clear furniture frantically, even snipe, and so precipitate the violence they fear.",
          "Minimize your own displacement activities; they read as aggression or stoke the patient's fear of losing control.",
          "Decrease eye contact.",
          "Keep the hands low, by the sides, palms up when gesturing, a primate signal of submission. Never behind the back, which suggests a hidden weapon.",
          "Stoop the shoulders slightly and bend the knees to lose height; attackers raise shoulders and chest.",
          "Stay in front; approaching from behind or the side startles.",
          "Give more space. Potentially violent patients have enlarged buffer zones and feel you are in their face at six feet. The impulse to calm by touching ends after the first encounter with feet or fists.",
          "In an unfamiliar emergency room, sit nearer the door than the patient without blocking their path to it. Pretending such moments do not arise is denial that prevents preparing for them."
        ] },
        { type: "p", text: "With sensible use of officers and medication, many encounters can be avoided. Helping a patient regain internal control is one of the fine points of the art, and it keeps the clinician around to practice it." }
      ],
      practice: [{ kind: "practice", id: "c8p3", label: "Practice: refusing the dominance reciprocal" }, { kind: "sim", id: "sim-pacing", label: "Interview: The man who won't sit" }]
    },

    {
      id: "technology",
      title: "Video, telephone, and text",
      lede: "On a screen you are a phantom; on the phone you are a voice; in a chat window you are naked words.",
      blocks: [
        { type: "p", text: "This part did not exist for the last edition. From his work with Lifeline's standards committee, Shea has watched the web open channels face-to-face care cannot: anonymity, which feels safe to someone contemplating suicide, cannot be traced, and may be the deciding factor in reaching out; and the native familiarity of chat, text, and messaging for the generation reading this book, leading to fully web-based crisis centers for veterans and students, whose clinicians then try to persuade the most dangerous contacts into a face-to-face assessment. Every such contact is an initial interview of a sort, with one shared feature: scarcity of nonverbal interaction. Most readers will also take crisis calls, cover colleagues by phone, and conduct video interviews and medication checks for rural patients." },
        { type: "p", text: "Interactive audio-visual technology (telepsychiatry by videoconference or Skype-like apps) serves the remote, the homebound, LGBT patients wary of local clinicians, prisoners, those needing off-hours care, and people who simply prefer distance and control (Zur). It shows faces, nods, and gestures, yet a day of lectures by videoconference is dull in a way the same speaker live is not. Immediacy explains it: proxemics requires a palpable presence in the room, and on a screen there is none. The clinician is a talking head, a phantom to the patient and vice versa: the [[Phantom presence effect]]. Consequences: you lose the patient's proxemic clues to blending (leaning in, keeping distance) and your own ability to move closer at a painful moment or yield to anger; eye contact weakens in both directions, and with it the reading of agreement in the closing. Rambo's fix: peering at the patient's eyes on screen looks, from their side, like gazing downward, so look into the camera at least every ten seconds. Shea speculates that the effect also reflects alertness. Immediacy presupposes a body in the room that might interact with you at any moment, which activates attention; on video that activation is absent, placing more weight on the words, so increase empathic statements. Some patients, socially anxious or paranoid, prefer video for exactly this reason. Expect your intuition to be compromised, especially for suicidal intent and deceit, and remember the image may be phone-sized, with gestures off-screen." },
        { type: "p", text: "The telephone strips everything but paralanguage. Its anonymity and distance help some callers open up (the bartender effect), but assessment is harder than on video: with facial expression, gaze, nods, lean, and gesture gone, intuition about suicidal intent, dangerousness, deceit, and real interest in follow-up should be trusted less once consulted, and formulation must lean more on analysis; if something does not add up, keep the call going and find out why. Learn the caller's paralanguage baseline early, while they talk freely, since it changes when sensitive material (incest, violence, drinking, suicidal thoughts) is being hidden; speech errors, repeated phrases, and longer RTL mark anxiety and deceit; silences are multiply determined and worth a gentle question. Train with back-to-back role plays. Engagement is harder too: the patient reads only your voice, so a terse voice makes a terse person and a fast voice a pushy one, and the visual immediacy behaviors that normally counterbalance an ordinary voice are gone. Record yourself and listen without the picture. Increase empathic statements, empathic grunting, and 'go on.' And be careful with humor: the visual cues that frame a joke are absent, so no kidding with angry, disengaged, or paranoid callers, and sarcasm misfires even with familiar patients." },
        { type: "p", text: "Chat, instant messaging, and texting remove the last vestige. Emoticons and acronyms are painfully inadequate substitutes. Reading the patient is hampered, intuition must be assessed with even more caution, and the clinician has no nonverbal tools at all: no nod, gaze, lean, or tone. The disquiet of being stripped of 60 to 65% of social meaning is the [[Naked communication effect]], truly unnerving during a suicide assessment. Silence takes new forms: a patient who has said there are pills in hand may not answer 'Are you thinking of overdosing right now?' for an hour, or may sign off; delays force interviewers to run several assessments at once, which is not everyone's cup of tea; and a slow patient may be multitasking, even asking friends what they think of the clinician's advice. One real benefit: a supervisor can be consulted throughout, with every earlier exchange retrievable verbatim. Early research (Zhou) finds that liars in instant messaging pause more briefly and correct themselves less, perhaps strategically, as Andersen suggests. The hope remains that web clinicians will bring difficult patients to a face-to-face interview, where the richness of two bodies in a room can do its work." },
        { type: "pearl", title: "Frankel's closing line", text: "Most physicians in training spend the early part of their careers interacting with books. The book does not care what facial expression you have while reading it. Patients care a lot. With that, Part I ends, and Part II turns to the disorders themselves, from depression and bipolar illness to psychosis, and how to interview so that patients can share their symptoms and their pain." }
      ],
      practice: [{ kind: "practice", id: "c8p6", label: "Practice: the phantom presence effect" }]
    }
  ],

  deeper: [
    { title: "Read in the book", text: "Hall's four distances in full, Morris's complete lists of displacement activities, self-intimacies, and the four eye cut-offs, the Ekman and Friesen nursing-student study, Scheflen's courting catalogue, Figure 8.1 (seating A, B, and C), Kuhnke's discordant signals, the full accounts of Mary, the three personality vignettes, the furrowed brow, and the parka exchange, and Shea's detailed reasoning on note taking and on each technology." },
    { title: "Chapters this one connects to", text: "Chapter 1 for blending, DOU and RTL, empathic valence, and the paranoid spiral, which immediacy valence mirrors; Chapter 3 for the shut-down interview and the closing; Chapter 5 for behavioral incidents, where nonverbal leakage of deceit is watched; Chapter 6 for kulturbrille; Chapter 11 for engaging paranoid process; Chapter 17 for the no-notes rule during suicide inquiry; Chapter 18 for the predictors of immediate assault; Chapter 20 for culture." },
    { title: "Sources Shea draws on", text: "Hall's The Hidden Dimension; Birdwhistell's Introduction to Kinesics; Scheflen on kinesic reciprocals; Ekman and Friesen on emblems, illustrators, regulators, adaptors, and affective displays, and Ekman's Emotions Revealed, Telling Lies, and the METT; Matsumoto, Frank, and Hwang's Nonverbal Communication; Andersen's textbook; Desmond Morris on displacement, self-intimacy, cut-offs, intention movements, and vacuum gestures; Grinder and Bandler's early work on paramessages; Kuhnke on discordant signals; Sue and Sue on cross-cultural distance, eye contact, and silence; Tardiff on violence; Zur, Rambo, Zhou, and the Sommers-Flanagans on technology." }
  ]
};
